{"lecture_id":"2026-06-23-M1-00","lecture_summary":"The lecture introduces rotational kinematics as the circular-motion analogue of translational kinematics: θ↔x, ω↔v, and α↔a; s=rθ, v=rω, and a_t=rα; radial versus tangential acceleration; and constant-angular-acceleration equations. Its representative example finds when a disk with ω(t)=a−bt² reverses direction and integrates ω(t) to obtain angular displacement, emphasizing that variable acceleration requires calculus. The broader problem-solving message is to model the system, state assumptions, choose representations, test equation applicability, and only then calculate.","videos":[{"rank":1,"title":"Physics With Calculus - Basic Introduction","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IQ4LGQRBhrc","reason":"Builds the derivative–integral foundation connecting position, velocity, and acceleration. This supports the lecture’s crucial strategy of differentiating or integrating time-dependent motion instead of using constant-acceleration formulas."},{"rank":2,"title":"Rotational Motion Physics, Basic Introduction, Angular Velocity & Tangential Acceleration","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WQ9AH2S8B6Y","reason":"Best conceptual match for translating linear motion into angular displacement, velocity, and acceleration, while relating angular quantities to tangential and centripetal acceleration."},{"rank":3,"title":"Rotational Kinematics Physics Problems, Basic Introduction, Equations & Formulas","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0El-DqrCTZM","reason":"Adds focused problem practice with angular displacement, velocity, acceleration, and time, covering the lecture’s constant-angular-acceleration equations and rotational-kinematics setup without duplicating the conceptual introduction."}],"coverage_gaps":["No strong playlist match addresses the lecture’s full physics-first workflow: defining the system, stating assumptions, drawing representations, and selecting equations before doing algebra.","No video explicitly matches the representative function ω(t)=a−bt², finding reversal from ω=0, and integrating it to obtain 72 rad; the selected videos collectively cover the required method.","The playlist descriptions do not show a strong focused treatment of radians, rotational sign conventions, or formal radial–tangential unit-vector notation.","The torque and angular-momentum equations are only brief previews in this lecture, so dedicated later-topic videos were excluded to keep the set minimal."]}
